                  "label": "Pub Title",
                  "value": "Haight-Ashbury"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Pub Note",
                  "value": "\"A vibrant, imaginative poster celebrating San Francisco’s iconic Haight-Ashbury neighborhood, created by artist David Wills and printed by the renowned psychedelic-era press Tea Lautrec Litho. Produced for Wills’s exhibition at the Off the Wall Gallery in 1982, the work blends contemporary artistic style with visual echoes of the district’s storied countercultural past. Set against a deep black background, the central panel presents a brilliantly colored, almost dreamlike montage of Haight-Ashbury landmarks rising from an island of Victorian houses surrounded by swirling water. A rainbow arcs across the left side of the sky, while lightning fractures the right—an interplay of harmony and turbulence that reflects the neighborhood’s history as both haven and crucible for artistic and social experimentation. Figures, musicians, and street life animate the scene, evoking the layered cultural memory of the Haight from the 1960s through the early 1980s. Below the image, “Haight-Ashbury” appears in bold purple type, followed by Wills’s signature and the exhibition imprint, “Off The Wall Gallery San Francisco 1982.” Printed by Tea Lautrec Litho, the legendary San Francisco workshop associated with rock-poster design, the poster connects two eras of local visual culture—psychedelic printmaking and early 1980s fine-art illustration. A striking and highly decorative tribute to one of San Francisco’s most emblematic neighborhoods, and a fine example of Tea Lautrec’s offset lithography in its later period.\" (Ruderman, @025)"
                },
